WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:03.661
There are 12 of them.

00:00:04.881 --> 00:00:07.623
12 stadiums built in the four corners of Europe.

00:00:10.964 --> 00:00:16.246
Conceived by the most daring architects and built by thousands of workers and engineers.

00:00:20.248 --> 00:00:25.131
12 gigantic arenas selected to host matches for a global event,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.472
the 2020 European Football Championship.

00:00:31.496 --> 00:00:36.585
Embark on a unique tour of Europe to discover the most beautiful stadiums on the continent.

00:01:02.871 --> 00:01:03.713
In this episode,

00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:10.216
The match will pit Wembley Stadium in London, England against the Allianz Arena in Munich, Germany.

00:01:28.768 --> 00:01:29.168
On one hand,

00:01:29.748 --> 00:01:32.489
a 90,000-seat stadium with a retractable roof,

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.410
supported by a huge arch.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.212
A stadium built in accordance with English traditions and a global benchmark in football.

00:01:41.373 --> 00:01:44.514
Pelé nicknamed it the cathedral of football.

00:01:44.914 --> 00:01:46.015
the football capital,

00:01:46.175 --> 00:01:47.155
the heart of football.

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:51.301
And on the other hand,

00:01:51.841 --> 00:01:53.923
an arena entirely dedicated to football

00:01:54.423 --> 00:01:56.625
where the viewer is placed as close as possible to the action.

00:01:57.344 --> 00:01:59.127
A stadium with an innovative design,

00:01:59.606 --> 00:02:01.789
covered with an inflatable cushion skin,

00:02:02.209 --> 00:02:03.891
capable of lighting up with a thousand lights.

00:02:05.171 --> 00:02:06.353
To illuminate the facade,

00:02:06.772 --> 00:02:09.435
We have a palette of 16 million colors,

00:02:09.836 --> 00:02:11.137
And that applies to all the diamond shapes.

00:02:12.978 --> 00:02:14.139
London and Munich,

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:16.221
The clash of two titans.

00:02:38.032 --> 00:02:39.954
Let's begin our journey in England,

00:02:40.394 --> 00:02:41.414
the homeland of football.

00:02:44.437 --> 00:02:46.759
About ten kilometers from central London,

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.822
Wembley Stadium stands prominently,

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:53.586
inaugurated in 2007 and completely rebuilt on the site of the old enclosure.

00:02:59.372 --> 00:02:59.793
Wembley,

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:02.155
a legendary stadium for an entire people.

00:03:03.376 --> 00:03:07.981
A reputation that goes far beyond borders and has reached a global dimension.

00:03:11.487 --> 00:03:15.472
Simon Inglis is a historian and leading expert on British football.

00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:20.057
He has written several books on the origins of this temple of football.

00:03:24.856 --> 00:03:28.399
Wembley Stadium exists because, after the First World War,

00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:37.644
Great Britain wanted to organize a large exhibition to celebrate peace and also to let the world know that its colonial adventure was still in full swing.

00:03:40.387 --> 00:03:43.409
It was really complicated to raise the necessary funds,

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:46.492
to interest the British public and investors.

00:03:46.852 --> 00:03:48.193
And in 1921,

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:48.794
they said to themselves,

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:50.175
Let's put a stadium here.

00:03:50.335 --> 00:03:51.656
Let's build a national stadium.

00:03:52.316 --> 00:03:55.899
There was this notion that England needed a stadium befitting the Empire.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:56.700
And obviously,

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.441
They called it the Empire Stadium.

00:04:05.923 --> 00:04:07.204
Built in barely a year,

00:04:07.764 --> 00:04:10.846
At the time, this stadium was the largest in the world.

00:04:11.326 --> 00:04:14.568
capable of accommodating up to 127,000 standing spectators.

00:04:17.711 --> 00:04:18.990
On the day of its inauguration,

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:20.431
The stadium is packed.

00:04:21.211 --> 00:04:24.274
Tens of thousands of ticketless fans invade the pitch.

00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:29.336
They have come to attend the most anticipated match of the year.

00:04:29.916 --> 00:04:31.437
the FA Cup final,

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:33.478
the English Cup (football).

00:04:35.915 --> 00:04:36.836
Over the decades,

00:04:37.478 --> 00:04:40.100
The Empire Stadium will set the rhythm of British life.

00:04:41.021 --> 00:04:43.463
The country's biggest events are held there.

00:04:44.044 --> 00:04:56.136
The 1948 Olympic Games and especially the 1966 FIFA World Cup.

00:04:56.156 --> 00:04:59.179
The 1966 World Cup,

00:04:59.180 --> 00:04:59.939
the only time...

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.141
The only time England,

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.403
the so-called home of football,

00:05:05.002 --> 00:05:07.144
actually wins the World Cup.

00:05:07.624 --> 00:05:08.204
And what's more,

00:05:08.545 --> 00:05:09.464
It happens at home.

00:05:10.586 --> 00:05:10.925
And that,

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:12.666
Nothing can take it away from us.

00:05:13.507 --> 00:05:14.548
For my generation,

00:05:15.428 --> 00:05:16.910
This is a key date in our lives.

00:05:18.149 --> 00:05:20.271
And we associate that with Wembley.

00:05:20.292 --> 00:05:21.512
66

00:05:21.952 --> 00:05:25.274
Wembley. It's part of our collective memory.

00:05:28.673 --> 00:05:30.074
From the 1990s onwards,

00:05:30.735 --> 00:05:32.557
The stadium's glory days are numbered.

00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:37.745
The leaders of the English Football Association want a more modern stadium.

00:05:40.248 --> 00:05:43.349
Five different cities have submitted bids to replace Wembley.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:44.151
Wembley,

00:05:44.211 --> 00:05:44.651
obviously,

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:45.510
then Bradford,

00:05:45.651 --> 00:05:46.171
Sheffield,

00:05:46.291 --> 00:05:47.632
Manchester and Birmingham.

00:05:48.093 --> 00:05:50.973
They all made an offer to host the new national stadium.

00:05:51.774 --> 00:05:53.656
For the first time since the 1920s,

00:05:54.096 --> 00:05:56.956
Wembley had to justify its importance to the entire nation.

00:05:58.557 --> 00:05:59.338
And that's what she did.

00:05:59.718 --> 00:06:01.819
The city of Wembley won the tender.

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:08.683
Especially because the football association saw only Wembley as a suitable venue for England team matches and cup finals.

00:06:08.903 --> 00:06:09.103
it's necessary

00:06:09.103 --> 00:06:10.245
It had to be in London.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:16.310
So this is it,

00:06:16.911 --> 00:06:18.853
on the very site of the former Wembley stadium,

00:06:19.394 --> 00:06:21.156
that the new enclosure now stands.

00:06:22.617 --> 00:06:25.120
A huge stadium with a capacity of 90,000,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.805
four times higher than the old arena and covering twice the area.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.122
It is the largest stadium in the country and one of the largest covered stadiums in the world.

00:06:40.242 --> 00:06:44.846
Access to the stands is via 36 entrances distributed all around the stadium.

00:06:46.007 --> 00:06:50.588
The facilities and services offered to supporters reflect the high standards of this football temple.

00:06:51.368 --> 00:06:51.889
excessive.

00:06:55.331 --> 00:07:01.694
No fewer than 98 kitchens supply the 688 fast food and beverage kiosks.

00:07:05.831 --> 00:07:07.432
There are about ten restaurants present.

00:07:07.932 --> 00:07:13.995
including some reserved exclusively for the 17,500 privileged members of the highly selective Club Wembley.

00:07:16.396 --> 00:07:23.319
The Royal family also has its own private entrances and elevators to watch matches from the Royal Box.

00:07:27.915 --> 00:07:29.557
But to reach such an extreme,

00:07:30.237 --> 00:07:34.400
The architects at Foster &amp; Partners had to overcome many challenges.

00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:40.764
The first was to design a modern venue while preserving its extraordinary heritage.

00:07:47.648 --> 00:07:50.069
The most famous feature of the old stadium,

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:52.871
It was his two masterful towers at the entrance.

00:07:55.424 --> 00:08:02.052
They had been the symbol of English football history for 80 years and their destruction shook the entire British people.

00:08:08.145 --> 00:08:09.986
When we started studying design,

00:08:10.547 --> 00:08:15.771
We were standing in front of a 75-year-old national monument with its two towers as symbols.

00:08:17.252 --> 00:08:19.314
They were approximately 35 meters tall.

00:08:19.874 --> 00:08:21.915
And when we wondered if we could keep them,

00:08:22.697 --> 00:08:25.079
We realized that the new stadium was really too big.

00:08:25.658 --> 00:08:28.161
We were forced to move them or even demolish them.

00:08:28.682 --> 00:08:31.463
One of the biggest challenges has been replacing this national icon.

00:08:31.903 --> 00:08:34.105
We needed something visually powerful.

00:08:40.552 --> 00:08:48.982
It was while searching for a way to support the colossal weight of the roof that the architects came up with the idea of ​​the element that might be able to replace the two towers in the hearts of the English.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.502
We studied the idea of ​​an arch extending over the roof.

00:08:58.162 --> 00:09:01.264
So we had to decide how to support the roof using the arch.

00:09:02.124 --> 00:09:04.926
And we were rather intrigued by the idea of ​​diagonal cables.

00:09:05.826 --> 00:09:09.168
And these cables that descend from the arch form a structure.

00:09:09.728 --> 00:09:10.589
If we look at it from a distance,

00:09:10.729 --> 00:09:11.849
It looks like a crown.

00:09:11.969 --> 00:09:12.590
a tiara.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:15.651
We thought it would create a very strong image of the stadium.

00:09:18.048 --> 00:09:23.451
The engineers will then create a steel structure 315 meters long and 135 meters high.

00:09:24.631 --> 00:09:31.456
An immense arch composed of 42 segments, each 7.5 meters in diameter and 10.4 meters long.

00:09:33.337 --> 00:09:35.399
They first assembled all the elements on the ground,

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:39.442
then fixed the two attachment points to the ground on each side of the arch.

00:09:42.245 --> 00:09:46.228
They then rotated the 1700-tonne arch over the enclosure.

00:09:51.332 --> 00:09:52.793
For architects and engineers,

00:09:53.414 --> 00:09:58.898
Assembling and installing the arch was one of the most delicate stages in the construction of the stadium.

00:10:03.653 --> 00:10:05.995
The arch was erected on the site.

00:10:06.115 --> 00:10:06.576
on the ground,

00:10:07.035 --> 00:10:07.956
for 10 months,

00:10:08.476 --> 00:10:11.778
then lifted above the lawn for 6 weeks,

00:10:12.098 --> 00:10:13.019
in 3 steps.

00:10:15.221 --> 00:10:16.861
First, at 30 degrees above the ground.

00:10:17.701 --> 00:10:19.201
60 degrees,

00:10:19.202 --> 00:10:23.003
100 degrees and finally 112 degrees.

00:10:23.722 --> 00:10:32.965
All of this was monitored and controlled by computer 24 hours a day to ensure that all connections were acting as expected.

00:10:35.925 --> 00:10:37.686
Once the arch is held in position,

00:10:38.245 --> 00:10:39.527
inclined at 112 degrees,

00:10:40.287 --> 00:10:45.008
A huge metal ring, one kilometer long, was placed around the perimeter wall.

00:10:48.063 --> 00:10:49.344
Starting from this metal ring,

00:10:49.884 --> 00:10:54.846
A series of beams were constructed above the stadium stands to form the roof structure.

00:10:58.047 --> 00:10:59.348
During this construction phase,

00:11:00.128 --> 00:11:04.910
Temporary towers were erected to hold the metal beams above the void.

00:11:07.847 --> 00:11:08.749
Once the roof is finished,

00:11:09.528 --> 00:11:16.493
The weight of the roof beams was transferred to the arch by means of a series of cables stretched between the arch and the northern part of the roof.

00:11:20.216 --> 00:11:20.717
In the end,

00:11:21.577 --> 00:11:25.880
The arch supports the entire weight of the northern part of the roof and also 60%

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:27.001
of the weight of the southern part.

00:11:28.602 --> 00:11:29.443
The 40%

00:11:29.444 --> 00:11:33.307
the remaining weight is supported by the main structure of the enclosure.

00:11:37.755 --> 00:11:43.759
The arch remains in permanent balance thanks to the balancing action between the north and south parts of the roof.

00:11:46.601 --> 00:11:51.745
A technical feat that makes it possible to avoid the use of support pillars inside the stadium.

00:11:59.591 --> 00:12:00.610
As an architect,

00:12:01.091 --> 00:12:06.575
I really appreciate the collaborative work that has been done between our teams and that of the other firm.

00:12:07.755 --> 00:12:10.418
I think it's a really complex work.

00:12:12.579 --> 00:12:13.220
First of all,

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:15.081
stands that are fixed,

00:12:15.882 --> 00:12:20.284
an arch that would fall if it weren't for the roof to keep it balanced,

00:12:22.225 --> 00:12:24.707
and a roof that is supported by the arch,

00:12:25.368 --> 00:12:27.188
which is itself under tension.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:32.993
A roof whose fixed sections protect the spectators,

00:12:33.493 --> 00:12:37.296
but which is also equipped with immovable elements to ensure safety.

00:12:41.599 --> 00:12:45.362
I think there is a real harmony that is quite exceptional.

00:12:50.949 --> 00:12:51.649
Originally,

00:12:51.788 --> 00:12:53.570
the stadium was known for its two towers.

00:12:54.551 --> 00:12:55.132
And now,

00:12:55.232 --> 00:12:56.692
130 meters wide,

00:12:57.093 --> 00:13:00.055
You can actually see it from over 60 kilometers away.

00:13:00.676 --> 00:13:00.875
SO,

00:13:01.115 --> 00:13:03.638
not only does it become a new symbol of the stadium,

00:13:04.399 --> 00:13:06.800
but it's something instantly recognizable.

00:13:07.641 --> 00:13:09.282
No other stadium in the world has that.

00:13:11.927 --> 00:13:13.588
As with Wembley Stadium in London,

00:13:14.209 --> 00:13:19.774
The distinctive architecture of a stadium can transform certain buildings into emblems of an entire city.

00:13:22.496 --> 00:13:25.399
One of the most striking examples is in Germany,

00:13:25.519 --> 00:13:26.119
in Munich,

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:27.681
capital of Bavaria.

00:13:29.863 --> 00:13:32.425
The city is home to one of the most beautiful stadiums in the world,

00:13:33.085 --> 00:13:33.906
the Allianz Arena.

00:13:43.916 --> 00:13:47.778
Renowned for its exceptional architecture and its dazzling white exterior,

00:13:48.279 --> 00:13:50.262
It is nicknamed the inflatable boat.

00:13:57.067 --> 00:14:01.031
This stadium was built in 2005 to house the city's two clubs.

00:14:01.591 --> 00:14:04.854
TSV 1860 and FC Bayern.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:14.221
Since 2017,

00:14:14.961 --> 00:14:17.782
It is the exclusive property of FC Bayern Munich.

00:14:18.363 --> 00:14:23.745
the most decorated club in the country and one of the biggest in Europe with 5 Champions League titles to its credit.

00:14:31.989 --> 00:14:34.030
But to build a new stadium north of Munich,

00:14:34.671 --> 00:14:36.411
Many challenges had to be overcome.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:38.200
The first,

00:14:38.701 --> 00:14:43.184
to convince local authorities of the need for a temple entirely dedicated to football.

00:14:48.989 --> 00:14:49.969
In the 1990s,

00:14:50.049 --> 00:14:55.674
The two federations already wanted to build a new football stadium in Munich.

00:14:56.734 --> 00:14:57.255
At the time,

00:14:57.375 --> 00:14:58.576
They were playing at the Olympia stadium.

00:15:00.751 --> 00:15:02.832
That is to say, in a stadium that is not a football stadium,

00:15:04.392 --> 00:15:06.033
but something completely different.

00:15:06.273 --> 00:15:07.013
A huge stadium,

00:15:07.113 --> 00:15:07.714
very open.

00:15:12.296 --> 00:15:14.657
It has a larger area and surrounds the football pitch,

00:15:14.717 --> 00:15:16.878
There is a 400-meter athletics track.

00:15:17.998 --> 00:15:19.779
The spectators are therefore very far removed from the game.

00:15:20.799 --> 00:15:22.980
It is also a stadium with only one row of stands,

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:24.661
That is to say, there is a very flat plane.

00:15:25.421 --> 00:15:27.082
So we are even further removed from the game.

00:15:28.423 --> 00:15:30.044
The distance between the spectators,

00:15:30.045 --> 00:15:35.629
The playing field and the players are significantly more important.

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:44.216
And it was from this observation that we had the idea of ​​building a real football stadium north of Munich, and not an Olympic stadium.

00:15:48.194 --> 00:15:49.234
In October 2001,

00:15:49.915 --> 00:15:52.355
Munich residents vote in favor of a new football stadium.

00:15:53.015 --> 00:15:56.977
despite the reluctance of the city council, which would prefer to renovate the old Olympic stadium.

00:16:00.458 --> 00:16:04.139
The construction project began in October 2002 and lasted two and a half years.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.130
At the time,

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.611
The architects and engineers had to overcome many challenges.

00:16:20.712 --> 00:16:21.532
The first of them,

00:16:22.092 --> 00:16:23.713
the location chosen for its construction.

00:16:29.455 --> 00:16:30.655
Located in the north of Munich,

00:16:30.895 --> 00:16:32.516
between the airport and the city center,

00:16:32.956 --> 00:16:35.717
The building is wedged between a railway line and a motorway.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:45.215
It was in this precise location that it was necessary to build not only a 171,000 m² stadium,

00:16:47.297 --> 00:16:49.619
but also a giant car park for spectators.

00:16:58.607 --> 00:17:01.730
The construction costs will be borne by the two clubs in the city.

00:17:06.974 --> 00:17:09.616
At the head of this monument, which has become one of the city's emblems,

00:17:10.277 --> 00:17:10.958
Jürgen Muth,

00:17:11.238 --> 00:17:15.021
stadium manager.

00:17:16.262 --> 00:17:20.546
This is one of the first stages that gave great importance to architecture.

00:17:22.188 --> 00:17:25.230
We worked with world-renowned architects,

00:17:25.511 --> 00:17:27.232
Jacques Herzog and Pierre de Meuron,

00:17:27.673 --> 00:17:30.575
who did not simply build a stadium out of concrete and steel,

00:17:31.116 --> 00:17:33.198
but above all a kind of envelope.

00:17:35.779 --> 00:17:38.280
This stadium has been featured on magazine covers.

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:39.400
And today,

00:17:39.921 --> 00:17:41.962
We are proud to see that the architects,

00:17:42.502 --> 00:17:49.885
but also the public attending the European Championship or fans from all over the world who come to Munich just to visit this building.

00:17:53.246 --> 00:17:54.127
According to Robert Hossel,

00:17:54.467 --> 00:17:56.028
project manager of the Allianz Arena,

00:17:56.768 --> 00:17:58.869
This stadium was not to resemble any other.

00:18:06.631 --> 00:18:13.155
We absolutely did not want to turn this roof and facade into a classical building.

00:18:13.695 --> 00:18:13.995
The idea,

00:18:14.075 --> 00:18:15.776
The goal was to create a curved object.

00:18:15.997 --> 00:18:17.397
all curved,

00:18:18.298 --> 00:18:20.679
which in a way closes in on itself.

00:18:20.739 --> 00:18:25.402
This geometry can therefore be found almost everywhere.

00:18:27.123 --> 00:18:30.204
Even on the facade with its cushions,

00:18:30.464 --> 00:18:32.325
which was placed somewhat like a cocoon,

00:18:32.906 --> 00:18:33.386
like a skin.

00:18:36.529 --> 00:18:36.850
NOW,

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:39.911
This facade and roof make this building a true object.

00:18:41.372 --> 00:18:50.438
An extraordinary object,

00:18:51.158 --> 00:18:53.920
symbol of a new era in stadium design.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.502
To achieve such an architectural feat,

00:19:08.263 --> 00:19:12.606
The engineers will propose an original material that has already proven its worth in construction.

00:19:13.287 --> 00:19:15.869
but until now never used for a football stadium.

00:19:18.251 --> 00:19:20.333
Ethylene tetrafluoroethylene,

00:19:20.933 --> 00:19:21.514
code name

00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:22.475
ETFE.

00:19:39.699 --> 00:19:42.261
This plastic material can be stretched into a film.

00:19:43.762 --> 00:19:44.663
By layering them,

00:19:45.364 --> 00:19:48.767
This creates pockets that can be inflated to form cushions.

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:07.724
The Alliance Arena envelope is therefore composed of exactly

00:20:08.245 --> 00:20:10.887
2742 plastic inflatable cushions.

00:20:18.256 --> 00:20:20.658
The assembly took 9 months of work.

00:20:31.148 --> 00:20:36.810
A giant puzzle where each diamond-shaped piece must fit perfectly with the others.

00:20:44.753 --> 00:20:50.035
Each cushion measures on average 6 meters by 3 meters and its plastic casing does not exceed

00:20:50.455 --> 00:20:52.456
0.25 mm thick.

00:20:54.676 --> 00:20:57.498
With a density of barely 350 grams per square meter,

00:20:58.659 --> 00:21:01.142
The cushion is ultra-light but very durable.

00:21:01.902 --> 00:21:04.544
It can support up to 400 times its own weight.

00:21:12.271 --> 00:21:17.315
The use of ETFE therefore considerably reduces the load on the roof structure.

00:21:24.184 --> 00:21:27.427
Tobias Sieber is the stadium maintenance manager.

00:21:28.727 --> 00:21:34.132
He takes us into the bowels of the Allianz Arena to reveal the secrets of its gigantic inflatable facade.

00:21:39.095 --> 00:21:42.638
Each cushion is constantly maintained under a pressure of 300 pascals.

00:21:46.857 --> 00:21:50.299
This pressure is generated by four air supply devices.

00:21:53.060 --> 00:22:02.886
The air is distributed through a very complex network of ducts that measures several kilometers.

00:22:04.307 --> 00:22:04.887
On the roof,

00:22:05.467 --> 00:22:06.428
There are sensors.

00:22:06.668 --> 00:22:10.211
which measure wind intensity and snow depth.

00:22:13.695 --> 00:22:22.323
This information is sent to the air supply system, which then automatically adjusts the pressure sent to the cushions.

00:22:23.724 --> 00:22:30.530
The pressure can reach up to 800 pascals and the cushions can support the weight of one meter of snow without any problem.

00:22:36.505 --> 00:22:37.886
Of course,

00:22:38.406 --> 00:22:41.868
Holes may form in the cushions.

00:22:42.448 --> 00:22:44.509
But thanks to the low pressure inside,

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:54.454
You can have a hole of 5 to 10 cm without the cushion deforming and eventually collapsing.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:00.561
Regularly,

00:23:01.081 --> 00:23:05.304
Stadium technicians access the roof to carry out inspection and maintenance visits.

00:23:13.091 --> 00:23:14.692
This material is really very strong.

00:23:14.852 --> 00:23:16.774
You can sit on it and even lie down on it.

00:23:17.034 --> 00:23:17.975
It holds up perfectly.

00:23:18.295 --> 00:23:18.756
That's great.

00:23:25.917 --> 00:23:26.458
Regularly,

00:23:26.498 --> 00:23:29.382
We come to check for the presence of holes and we repair them.

00:23:32.907 --> 00:23:34.048
During the design of the stadium...

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.733
The engineers had to overcome another challenge.

00:23:37.814 --> 00:23:40.699
What should be done in case of a complete shutdown of the ventilation system?

00:23:42.763 --> 00:23:47.070
In case of a ventilation system failure...

00:23:48.452 --> 00:23:53.455
Large quantities of water could accumulate on the roof.

00:23:53.935 --> 00:23:59.498
This would potentially represent several hundred tons that the roof would have to support.

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:07.242
And the cushions could very well collapse under the weight of all that rain.

00:24:11.881 --> 00:24:17.464
An ingenious drainage system was then devised to prevent the roof from collapsing under the weight of the water.

00:24:21.507 --> 00:24:24.389
Each roof cushion is equipped with a long hollow tube.

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:28.251
In the event of a failure of the emergency ventilation systems,

00:24:28.792 --> 00:24:32.814
The cushions will deflate and water will then begin to fill the bottom.

00:24:36.248 --> 00:24:38.629
But the liquid cannot accumulate in large quantities,

00:24:39.609 --> 00:24:42.410
because it is gradually evacuated through the tubes.

00:24:44.791 --> 00:24:49.292
This ingenious system makes it possible to considerably reduce the load on the roof structure.

00:24:56.554 --> 00:24:57.494
Apart from the rain,

00:24:57.795 --> 00:25:01.276
The heat of the sun is another threat to the roof of the Allianz Arrila.

00:25:05.980 --> 00:25:07.681
Because of its permeability to UV light,

00:25:08.301 --> 00:25:13.302
The TFE allows a large amount of light and energy to pass through, which accumulates under the roof.

00:25:15.803 --> 00:25:20.484
It's a bit like a greenhouse effect where it's much hotter inside than outside.

00:25:21.144 --> 00:25:24.685
It can reach up to 80 or 90 degrees in summer.

00:25:29.087 --> 00:25:30.327
To regulate the temperature,

00:25:31.147 --> 00:25:33.608
The engineers therefore created several ventilation openings.

00:25:34.124 --> 00:25:35.505
which allow heat to dissipate.

00:25:38.326 --> 00:25:41.707
They are evenly distributed around the outer ring of the roof.

00:25:46.189 --> 00:25:46.989
Despite the heat,

00:25:47.469 --> 00:25:50.651
Sunlight is a determining factor in the design of a stadium.

00:25:51.791 --> 00:25:57.533
The architects must orient the stadium so that the players and spectators do not have the sun in their eyes during a match.

00:26:00.275 --> 00:26:03.536
They also need to think about the lawn, which needs light to grow.

00:26:07.538 --> 00:26:09.260
To flood the stadium's grass with sunlight,

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:15.123
The architects of the Alliance Arena came up with the idea of ​​using two types of cushions at the roof level.

00:26:17.885 --> 00:26:20.067
The cushions located to the north and northeast of the stadium,

00:26:20.467 --> 00:26:21.548
little exposure to the sun,

00:26:22.008 --> 00:26:27.412
are tinted white in order to reflect as much light as possible and reduce heat under the roof.

00:26:31.408 --> 00:26:33.870
Those located to the south and southwest of the stadium,

00:26:34.311 --> 00:26:35.472
very exposed to the sun

00:26:36.112 --> 00:26:41.036
They are transparent to allow the penetration of solar radiation necessary for the proper growth of the grass.

00:26:47.042 --> 00:26:47.762
During matches,

00:26:48.343 --> 00:26:53.667
Large grey curtains are drawn under these transparent cushions to limit the sun on the playing field.

00:27:04.352 --> 00:27:04.953
In London too,

00:27:05.493 --> 00:27:07.695
The architects had to work around the sun.

00:27:09.337 --> 00:27:18.206
They had to maintain the east-west orientation of the pitch in order to preserve the soul of the old Wembley and ensure that on the day of the FA Cup final,

00:27:18.786 --> 00:27:20.408
The lawn is in full sun.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:36.620
This orientation is ideal for avoiding shaded areas during the broadcast of the match and provides maximum sunlight to the pitch.

00:27:48.585 --> 00:27:55.471
But the International Federation of Football requires that a roof be placed over all seats during international competitions.

00:27:58.093 --> 00:28:01.115
A new constraint for architects to take into account.

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:05.461
We are faced with this kind of dilemma.

00:28:06.361 --> 00:28:09.142
FIFA requires us to cover all the seats in the stadium.

00:28:09.762 --> 00:28:13.103
But since the stadium has 90,000 seats and the stands are so high,

00:28:13.803 --> 00:28:17.104
This means that the opening in the middle of the roof would be very small.

00:28:17.724 --> 00:28:18.965
So the lawn would die.

00:28:21.646 --> 00:28:23.226
To maximize the amount of light on the lawn,

00:28:23.886 --> 00:28:28.688
The engineers devised a clever system of retractable and stackable panels.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.401
The aluminum roof is divided into 7 movable panels.

00:28:34.842 --> 00:28:36.282
3 panels located on the east side,

00:28:37.343 --> 00:28:40.724
3 on the west side and a huge sign to the south.

00:28:44.186 --> 00:28:46.887
The opening always starts with the first 2 panels from the bottom.

00:28:48.308 --> 00:28:48.868
As we reverse,

00:28:49.468 --> 00:28:51.309
these will draw the latter into their race

00:28:51.689 --> 00:28:52.089
3rd panel.

00:28:56.211 --> 00:28:58.112
The same process takes place on the west side.

00:29:00.168 --> 00:29:00.488
Finally,

00:29:01.308 --> 00:29:04.749
The largest panel located to the south can also be removed.

00:29:08.851 --> 00:29:09.651
Once opened,

00:29:10.151 --> 00:29:13.072
The entire surface of the lawn is thus bathed in sunlight.

00:29:19.014 --> 00:29:21.634
Ian Stevenson is in charge of the roofing.

00:29:24.215 --> 00:29:26.236
We find him again at the moment the roof is opened.

00:29:30.981 --> 00:29:31.622
Okay Dave,

00:29:31.983 --> 00:29:34.529
The start-up checks are carried out to the west.

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:36.073
The hatches are closed.

00:29:36.715 --> 00:29:38.579
The signs are ready when you're ready.

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:39.301
I repeat,

00:29:39.361 --> 00:29:40.203
Ready when you're ready.

00:29:42.013 --> 00:29:42.153
Okay,

00:29:42.294 --> 00:29:43.395
well received.

00:29:43.396 --> 00:29:45.076
We are beginning to open the S section of the roof.

00:29:45.517 --> 00:29:46.077
I repeat.

00:29:46.858 --> 00:29:48.139
Opening of section S of the roof,

00:29:48.199 --> 00:29:48.520
finished.

00:29:51.823 --> 00:29:52.283
Well received.

00:29:54.986 --> 00:29:59.611
The alarm sounds to indicate that the systems are running and that the roof is retracting.

00:29:59.851 --> 00:30:00.492
Here we go.

00:30:05.471 --> 00:30:11.203
So we're just going to head towards the drive unit and we're just going to observe and monitor the retracting roof.

00:30:12.065 --> 00:30:13.287
All the panels at once.

00:30:29.593 --> 00:30:29.714
There,

00:30:30.154 --> 00:30:32.016
This is the widest panel on the east side.

00:30:32.436 --> 00:30:33.797
the top panel number 1,

00:30:34.017 --> 00:30:35.679
which arrives in its final position.

00:30:36.179 --> 00:30:36.860
Those sensors,

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:37.600
For example,

00:30:37.961 --> 00:30:39.262
are located on the tracks.

00:30:39.722 --> 00:30:43.085
These sensors allow the movement to be triggered or stopped.

00:30:43.466 --> 00:30:50.171
They are coordinated with the central computer which controls the roof and thus commands its stopping in the final position.

00:30:57.421 --> 00:30:58.484
Thanks to all these sensors,

00:30:59.146 --> 00:31:04.961
The teams in the control room can track in real time the speed and position of each part of the moving roof.

00:31:16.246 --> 00:31:16.506
Here,

00:31:16.906 --> 00:31:20.588
We can see that there are different alarms and warning lights.

00:31:22.109 --> 00:31:26.152
They activate when there is a problem with any part of the roof.

00:31:27.192 --> 00:31:28.493
If there is something blocking it,

00:31:29.274 --> 00:31:31.595
if there is a strong gust of wind that could misalign the roof,

00:31:32.355 --> 00:31:33.016
The roof stops.

00:31:35.217 --> 00:31:35.537
And that,

00:31:35.918 --> 00:31:37.819
We'll see it instantly on this screen.

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:40.420
It will flash.

00:31:41.961 --> 00:31:44.444
And I need to understand what the lights or alarms indicate.

00:31:45.665 --> 00:31:51.531
then manage the problem and restart the opening or closing of the roof depending on what we are doing.

00:31:54.494 --> 00:31:55.015
In the end,

00:31:55.656 --> 00:32:03.223
No less than 57 minutes are needed to move the panels on the east and west sides and 17 minutes for the south panel.

00:32:05.437 --> 00:32:07.538
If the roof protects against inclement weather and sun,

00:32:08.358 --> 00:32:11.799
It also plays an unexpected but well-known role for architects.

00:32:12.739 --> 00:32:14.940
It contributes to the propagation of sound.

00:32:19.341 --> 00:32:27.964
Old Wembley was renowned worldwide for its deafening atmosphere, particularly the roar of the fans when the players entered the pitch.

00:32:29.384 --> 00:32:31.565
It was called the Wembley Raw.

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:44.792
One of the most amazing things about old Wembley,

00:32:45.332 --> 00:32:48.194
It was the sound swirling inside the enclosure.

00:32:48.714 --> 00:32:50.996
Hamden Park has something very similar,

00:32:51.336 --> 00:32:52.497
called the Hamden Roar.

00:32:53.077 --> 00:32:54.658
And that simply comes down to the fact that,

00:32:55.018 --> 00:32:55.999
when you have 100,000...

00:32:56.059 --> 00:33:14.351
With people in the stands, the acoustics bounced everywhere, and in 1963 they installed roofs to close off the stadium, which increased the noise level. It was a super noisy place, and that really helps during a football match.

00:33:18.529 --> 00:33:20.511
To recreate this very special atmosphere,

00:33:21.211 --> 00:33:25.395
The architects analyzed the behavior of sound in the old Wembley stadium.

00:33:26.976 --> 00:33:30.900
The results allowed them to improve the acoustics inside the new venue.

00:33:37.126 --> 00:33:40.868
We see some stadiums that have spaces between the back of the stands and the roof.

00:33:41.609 --> 00:33:42.429
If there is a space,

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:43.790
The sound exits through this space.

00:33:44.911 --> 00:33:45.912
The space was closed off.

00:33:46.052 --> 00:33:48.053
A strip of glass was put all around the stadium.

00:33:49.014 --> 00:33:49.434
And like that,

00:33:49.714 --> 00:33:51.135
The sound really fills the entire interior.

00:33:51.135 --> 00:33:53.458
inside the enclosure and reverberates throughout the stadium.

00:33:54.299 --> 00:33:55.240
So we get an echo.

00:33:57.022 --> 00:34:05.793
Spectators feel the atmosphere much more because the sound bounces off the roof and glass walls at the back of the stands and comes back onto the pitch into the players' ears.

00:34:06.914 --> 00:34:08.676
They can also hear the lower stand.

00:34:09.156 --> 00:34:12.580
We realized that the sounds from the lower stands reverberate throughout the stadium.

00:34:12.841 --> 00:34:15.223
but they also arrive directly at lawn level.

00:34:15.744 --> 00:34:17.726
So when you have a very large lower stand,

00:34:18.066 --> 00:34:19.568
That's what players hear most often.

00:34:19.909 --> 00:34:22.111
It makes them feel better and that's important for them.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:28.110
It's a real success.

00:34:28.851 --> 00:34:31.193
Even when Wembley is not completely full,

00:34:31.853 --> 00:34:34.835
We know that the atmosphere is as it was in the past,

00:34:35.316 --> 00:34:36.477
and even better, in fact.

00:34:37.177 --> 00:34:40.199
The viewers enjoy a better quality experience.

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:44.783
They come and are transported into a unique experience.

00:34:53.293 --> 00:34:54.273
Create an atmosphere,

00:34:55.134 --> 00:34:58.014
to bring the fans as close to the action as possible,

00:34:58.615 --> 00:35:03.056
has become a trademark and a guarantee of quality for the biggest football stadiums.

00:35:12.499 --> 00:35:15.039
For the architects at Herzog &amp; de Meuron,

00:35:15.960 --> 00:35:19.081
These elements were a priority from the very beginning of the stadium's design.

00:35:27.258 --> 00:35:33.552
The instruction for the construction of the Allianz Arena was to create a stadium specifically designed for football.

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:44.573
where fans are in direct contact with the players.

00:35:45.453 --> 00:35:47.934
There can even be genuine interaction during the match.

00:35:49.054 --> 00:35:52.135
The spectators are very close to the playing field.

00:35:54.776 --> 00:35:57.296
There are three levels of seating,

00:35:57.736 --> 00:36:01.297
The fans really dominate the game and the stadium interior is more compact.

00:36:01.517 --> 00:36:03.218
more focused on the football match.

00:36:07.981 --> 00:36:10.125
To bring the audience as close to the action as possible,

00:36:10.666 --> 00:36:15.575
The architects meticulously worked on the curvature of the enclosure and the layout of the stands.

00:36:17.919 --> 00:36:24.605
The challenge was to guarantee each visitor the best visibility and an unobstructed view from any point in the stands.

00:36:28.068 --> 00:36:28.468
For that,

00:36:29.129 --> 00:36:37.917
The architects had to ensure that a spectator could see action on the field without being obstructed by the head of the person sitting in front of him.

00:36:40.742 --> 00:36:46.347
This spectator's line of sight should not be obstructed by the head of the person in front of him.

00:36:48.468 --> 00:36:49.749
The same applies to the downstairs neighbor.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:53.512
whose line of sight should not be disturbed by its neighbor below.

00:36:57.315 --> 00:37:02.840
The architects therefore defined a minimum distance to be maintained between the lines of sight of the spectators.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:06.323
in order to guarantee them maximum visual comfort.

00:37:10.762 --> 00:37:11.983
The greater this distance,

00:37:12.943 --> 00:37:14.263
The better the visibility.

00:37:18.965 --> 00:37:19.205
So,

00:37:19.246 --> 00:37:24.948
Spectators in the lower stands of the Alliance Arena have a line of sight separated by an average of 10 cm.

00:37:27.969 --> 00:37:29.710
While on the intermediate stand,

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:32.671
The visibility lines are spaced 9 cm apart.

00:37:34.312 --> 00:37:35.372
In the upper tier,

00:37:35.652 --> 00:37:36.813
they are separated by on average

00:37:37.153 --> 00:37:37.653
8 cm.

00:37:40.158 --> 00:37:43.520
To offer greater visibility to spectators in the upper tier,

00:37:44.161 --> 00:37:47.443
The architects did not hesitate to tilt the grandstand to its maximum angle.

00:37:48.063 --> 00:37:50.745
that is, 34 degrees as permitted by regulations.

00:38:00.352 --> 00:38:04.034
But the atmosphere of a sporting event is not only created during the match itself.

00:38:05.415 --> 00:38:08.397
It begins as soon as the public arrives near the stadium.

00:38:13.433 --> 00:38:15.475
To accommodate the 70,000 fans,

00:38:16.035 --> 00:38:21.460
The architects of the Alliance Arena envisioned a huge esplanade overlooking a parking lot.

00:38:31.608 --> 00:38:38.934
The challenge was enormous because we had to create 11,000 spaces for what was going to be the largest covered car park in Europe.

00:38:39.302 --> 00:38:40.042
in a stadium.

00:38:43.483 --> 00:38:46.924
Rather than building a huge, invisible underground car park beneath the stadium,

00:38:47.844 --> 00:38:56.067
The architects therefore designed an esplanade surrounding the enclosure, under which different levels were built to accommodate vehicles.

00:38:57.907 --> 00:39:00.728
600 meters long and 134 meters wide,

00:39:01.428 --> 00:39:07.150
The esplanade rises to a height of 12 meters and then descends slightly to the level of the second floor of the stadium.

00:39:09.762 --> 00:39:13.608
This brilliant idea completely transformed spectator access to the stadium.

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:16.292
Like a modern-day cathedral,

00:39:16.933 --> 00:39:21.680
The enclosure gradually reveals itself to them as their hatred intensifies.

00:39:28.983 --> 00:39:29.803
On match days,

00:39:30.204 --> 00:39:32.145
The Esplanade is where it all begins.

00:39:32.885 --> 00:39:34.266
It must be a welcoming place,

00:39:35.227 --> 00:39:38.289
so that the suspense gradually builds among the fans,

00:39:38.890 --> 00:39:44.313
until the moment they all find themselves inside the stadium to watch a good football match.

00:39:52.699 --> 00:39:55.461
The Esplanade offers a place where the staging can begin.

00:39:58.655 --> 00:40:00.916
The Allianz Arena needs the esplanade,

00:40:01.476 --> 00:40:04.257
and the esplanade is the backdrop for the stadium and the game.

00:40:08.840 --> 00:40:10.020
In this game of staging,

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:15.763
The architects thought big and also devised an ingenious lighting system.

00:40:16.824 --> 00:40:20.906
Undoubtedly the one that made the Allianz Arena famous throughout the world.

00:40:23.051 --> 00:40:27.315
What's special about this stadium,

00:40:27.856 --> 00:40:31.600
That's because it was the headquarters of two football clubs.

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:36.145
Two clubs that therefore have different identities,

00:40:36.465 --> 00:40:37.686
different fans.

00:40:38.307 --> 00:40:43.432
Therefore, a stadium was needed that would serve as an identifying element for both clubs.

00:40:48.285 --> 00:40:50.807
It was a big challenge not to build a neutral stadium.

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:55.932
So I had the idea to solve this problem,

00:40:56.513 --> 00:40:58.214
these different identities,

00:40:59.335 --> 00:41:01.738
using colored lights.

00:41:04.220 --> 00:41:05.541
That is to say, to build a stadium which,

00:41:05.681 --> 00:41:06.682
a bit like a chameleon,

00:41:07.623 --> 00:41:09.985
can change identity depending on the team that is playing.

00:41:10.932 --> 00:41:15.777
Lyon 1860 or FC Bayern?

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:19.882
To create this play of light,

00:41:20.342 --> 00:41:27.170
The engineers placed a series of panels composed of 285 red LEDs at the base of each cushion.

00:41:27.190 --> 00:41:28.691
green and yellow which,

00:41:28.891 --> 00:41:29.672
once mixed,

00:41:30.093 --> 00:41:32.375
can provide a palette of 16 million colors.

00:41:40.084 --> 00:41:42.086
Thanks to its transparency characteristics,

00:41:42.626 --> 00:41:46.148
The TFE allows light to pass through and diffuses it outwards.

00:41:46.929 --> 00:41:49.871
giving the cushion a perfectly uniform color.

00:41:55.175 --> 00:41:55.655
In total,

00:41:56.376 --> 00:42:01.099
300,000 LEDs illuminate all the cushions surrounding the facade of the Alliance Arena.

00:42:17.204 --> 00:42:19.446
Since the departure of the TSV 1860,

00:42:20.066 --> 00:42:24.250
The arena now shines in red, the colours of FC Bayern Munich.

00:42:43.180 --> 00:42:45.162
Thanks to the shape of the cushions and the lighting,

00:42:45.823 --> 00:42:48.186
It is possible to recreate all sorts of patterns,

00:42:49.547 --> 00:42:50.809
including the club's famous logo,

00:42:51.269 --> 00:42:54.754
which proudly displays the blue and white diamonds of the Bavarian flag.

00:43:10.048 --> 00:43:11.969
At the helm of this multicolored game,

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:13.710
Christian Oussalman,

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:15.451
technical director of the arena.

00:43:17.612 --> 00:43:19.874
We are in the Alliance Arena control center.

00:43:21.034 --> 00:43:22.195
the nerve center.

00:43:23.716 --> 00:43:25.517
This is where we can control everything.

00:43:25.717 --> 00:43:26.757
including the facade.

00:43:29.019 --> 00:43:30.720
On World Diabetes Day,

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:31.920
We illuminate it in blue.

00:43:32.521 --> 00:43:33.221
During the week,

00:43:33.281 --> 00:43:34.322
when there is no match,

00:43:34.462 --> 00:43:38.384
We activate an animation that alternates between red and white every three minutes.

00:43:39.044 --> 00:43:43.867
We can also set up special events like during Independence Day with the Stars and Stripes.

00:43:44.827 --> 00:43:46.308
In green for St. Patrick's Day.

00:43:48.529 --> 00:43:50.631
Or in the colours of Christopher Street Day.

00:43:52.232 --> 00:43:55.714
Or even in orange for the King of Holland's holiday.

00:43:55.774 --> 00:43:58.536
In general,

00:43:58.716 --> 00:44:00.137
for international teams,

00:44:00.237 --> 00:44:01.658
The facade is illuminated in white.

00:44:02.259 --> 00:44:04.620
But you can also display the colors of the national flag.

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:11.362
Every night of the year,

00:44:11.782 --> 00:44:16.170
The Alliance Arena shines like a beacon in the middle of the Bavarian plain.

00:44:24.584 --> 00:44:26.166
